/*
    UIParty.uss
    Layout for the party panel. Colours come from FishMMO-Theme.uss; the buff
    icons reuse the .buff-group classes from UIBuffContainer.uss.

    The panel is a roster of GROUPS rather than of lines. Each member is one
    horizontal band: who they are, the three resources stacked so their relative
    heights read at a glance, what they are contributing, and what is currently
    being done to them. Reading down a column compares the party on one axis;
    reading across a band tells you everything about one player.

    The column strip matters more here than in most lists — three unlabelled
    bars of different colours are just a stack of rectangles.
*/

/*
    Anchored explicitly, in the left column below the target frame. Each panel is its own
    UIDocument whose root fills the screen, so a root with a size but no position resolves to the
    top-left corner.

    BOTH EDGES PINNED, so the frame occupies exactly the space that is free rather than a height
    of its own. It used to be `top: 50%; translate: 0 -50%` — vertically centred — which is the
    one place that cannot work: the chat panel is anchored to the bottom and gives ground
    downward as the window shortens, so the two met in the middle. Measured at 1200x800 they
    overlapped by 420x1.5px, and the overlap is 64px at 16:9 (a 675-unit panel) and grows from
    there as the screen gets wider. So the roster fills the gap instead: top at 129px, which is
    8px under the target frame, and bottom at 368px, which is the toast stack's offset — the
    frame ends where the toasts begin.

    The target frame is what sets 129 now, and it is a hard limit rather than a preference. It is
    centred and 536 wide, so it spans x332..868, and this frame's right edge at x454 is a long way
    under it. The columns only stop crossing if the roster is narrowed to 300 — measured, and a
    quarter of its width given up for it — so the height is what gives way instead. That was one
    of the two defects this 129 replaced: at 8 it overlapped the target frame by 122x73px in every
    buff/debuff state, because the frame had been free to take the corner the buff strip vacated.

    It was 144 before that, which was 8px under the buff strip's four rows in this same corner.
    The strip has moved to the bottom centre, and 129 is the 15 units that came back — the frame
    cannot start higher than y121 whatever else happens. Past that the column is booked solid by
    bottom-anchored panels: the chat log's top edge is 354 up and the toast stack sits 6px above
    it, so on a 21:9 panel, 506 units tall, there is nothing left for a roster and this frame is
    squeezed to a sliver. The remedy for that is the panel's match mode — PanelSettings matches
    width, so the panel is 1200 units wide at every aspect ratio and its height is whatever the
    screen gives — and that is a pass over every HUD panel rather than a number here.

    It is the internal scroll that absorbs this. .party-scroll already flex-grows with a
    min-height, so a frame given 288 units on a 16:10 screen shows more of a six-member party
    than one given 163 on a 16:9 screen, and neither ever collides with anything.

    Wider than the roster it replaced because it now carries four columns instead of three
    narrow ones. Sized against the 1200x800 reference resolution in PanelSettings.asset, so this
    is a little over a third of the screen's width — which is what a frame carrying six members'
    buffs needs, and why the effects column is the one that flexes.

    430 rather than 450. At left:2% (24 units of the 1200 the panel is always wide) that puts the
    right edge at x454, which is what clears .arenahud-root — centred, 260 wide, so x470..730 —
    by 16px. At 450 the two overlapped by 4x58px for as long as a party was in an arena, which
    is every arena match with a party in it.
*/
.party-panel {
    position: absolute;
    left: 2%;
    top: 129px;
    bottom: 368px;
    width: 430px;
    max-height: 72%;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ── Header ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */

.party-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 44px;
    padding-left: 10px;
    padding-right: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-header__icon {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    margin-right: 9px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-header__titles {
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.party-count {
    min-width: 34px;
    height: 16px;
    padding-left: 5px;
    padding-right: 5px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Sizing only — .fish-close-btn carries the colours, border and hover. */
.party-close-btn {
    margin-left: 8px;
}

/*
    A stacked title + subtitle needs its two line boxes pinned. Unity gives a
    Label a generous intrinsic height, so an unconstrained pair measures taller
    than the header that contains it — the subtitle then overflows into
    whatever sits below the header, which is exactly what it did.
*/
.party-header__titles > .fish-panel__title {
    height: 15px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-header__titles > .fish-panel__subtitle {
    height: 12px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ── Column strip ───────────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    The four widths below are repeated on the member row's four groups. They
    have to agree or the captions stop naming the thing under them, and USS has
    no variables for lengths — only for colours — so the pairing is maintained
    by hand and marked here.
*/
.party-columns {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 18px;
    padding-left: 12px;
    padding-right: 10px;
    background-color: var(--abyss-850);
    border-bottom-color: var(--abyss-400);
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/*
    Shared caption sizing; the modifiers below set the widths.

    Margin and padding are zeroed explicitly. Unity's runtime theme gives a
    Label its own margin, which the member row's four groups do not have — so
    with the same widths on both, every caption still drifted a few pixels
    further right than the column under it, and by the fourth one "EFFECTS" sat
    over the gap rather than over the icons. Measured, not guessed: the strip
    and the row now report identical x positions for all four columns.
*/
.party-col {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

.party-col--identity { width: 92px; }
.party-col--bars     { width: 126px; }
.party-col--meters   { width: 62px; }
.party-col--effects  { flex-grow: 1; flex-shrink: 1; width: auto; }

/* ── Body ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */

.party-scroll {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 64px;
}

.party-member-list {
    flex-direction: column;
}

/*
    One roster band. The theme's .fish-row supplies the hover state and the
    leading accent rail; this only sets the geometry.

    Height follows from the bars: three 16px tracks and two 2px gaps is 52px,
    plus 3px of breathing room top and bottom. Fixed rather than derived so six
    members always occupy the same space and the panel does not resize as buffs
    come and go.
*/
.party-member {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 58px;
    padding-left: 10px;
    padding-right: 10px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ── Member: identity ───────────────────────────────────── */

.party-member__identity {
    width: 92px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-right: 6px;
}

.party-member__name {
    height: 14px;
    overflow: hidden;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/*
    The rank marker is a badge rather than the bare asterisk the old row used.
    An asterisk in a monochrome column is a footnote mark; leadership is the one
    fact on this row that decides who a player has to ask for an invite or a
    kick, and it should not have to be decoded.

    A plain member's badge is made INVISIBLE rather than removed from layout.
    Removing it lets the identity column collapse to the height of the name
    alone, so the leader's name sits a few pixels higher than everyone else's
    and the one column a player scans down is the one column that is not
    aligned. An invisible badge draws nothing and keeps every name on the same
    baseline; C# sets visibility, not display, for the same reason.
*/
.party-member__rank {
    height: 13px;
    margin-top: 3px;
    padding-left: 5px;
    padding-right: 5px;
    align-self: flex-start;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ── Member: resource bars ──────────────────────────────── */

.party-member__bars {
    width: 126px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-right: 8px;
}

/*
    16px tall, stacked, health above mana above stamina. Tall enough that a
    glance across six members compares depth rather than hue, which is the whole
    reason a party frame has bars instead of numbers.
*/
.party-bar {
    height: 16px;
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-bar--mana,
.party-bar--stamina {
    margin-top: 2px;
}

/* Driven from C#: only the width moves. */
.party-bar__fill {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 100%;
}

.party-bar__label {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    font-size: 9px;
}

/* ── Member: output meters ──────────────────────────────── */

.party-member__meters {
    width: 62px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-right: 8px;
}

.party-meter {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 12px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-meter--heal {
    margin-top: 4px;
}

.party-meter__caption {
    width: 22px;
    font-size: 8px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: var(--abyss-300);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-meter__value {
    flex-grow: 1;
    font-size: 10px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.party-meter__value--damage { color: var(--hp); }
.party-meter__value--heal   { color: var(--stam); }

/*
    An idle meter is drawn in the muted text colour rather than hidden. A blank
    cell reads as "no data" when the true statement is "nothing yet this
    encounter", and the difference matters when the number is what a player is
    watching to decide whether somebody is contributing.
*/
.party-meter__value--idle {
    color: var(--abyss-300);
    -unity-font-style: normal;
}

/* ── Member: buffs and debuffs ──────────────────────────── */

/*
    To the right of the bars and given all the remaining width, because this is
    the only column whose content has no fixed size.
*/
.party-member__effects {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/*
    One strip of buffs above one strip of debuffs, matching the target frame,
    so a defensive cooldown can never be pushed off screen by a long list of
    debuffs. Both reuse .buff-list from UIBuffContainer.uss; the height caps
    each at a single row, which is what keeps a member with fifteen effects from
    resizing the band.
*/
.party-effects__strip {
    height: 22px;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-effects__strip--debuff {
    margin-top: 3px;
}

/*
    Party icons are a third of the size of the target frame's, because six
    members' worth of them share one panel. .buff-group's own margins are kept
    so the two strips still read as the same component.
*/
.party-effects__strip > .buff-group {
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    margin-right: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.party-effects__strip > .buff-group > .buff-group__label {
    font-size: 7px;
}

/* ── Member: out of zone ────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    A member on another scene server, in another zone, or in a different dungeon
    instance. The server sends no live state for them at all — see
    PartyMemberVitalsUpdateBroadcast — so what is on screen is the last thing
    this client was told, which may be minutes old.

    Drawn as a greyscale facade rather than hidden or blanked. The row still has
    to hold its place (the party has six members whether or not you can see them
    all) and the last known values are still worth something; what must not
    happen is the panel presenting them in the same colours as a member standing
    next to you, because those colours are a claim that the numbers are live.

    Every colour is restated here rather than relying on opacity alone. Fading
    the band would make it dimmer without making it any less colour-coded, and a
    dim red bar still reads as "that player is nearly dead right now".
*/
.party-member--away {
    opacity: 0.72;
}

.party-member--away .fish-bar__fill--hp   { background-color: var(--hp-muted); }
.party-member--away .fish-bar__fill--mp   { background-color: var(--mp-muted); }
.party-member--away .fish-bar__fill--stam { background-color: var(--stam-muted); }

.party-member--away .party-member__name  { color: var(--abyss-300); }
.party-member--away .party-bar__label    { color: var(--abyss-200); }
.party-member--away .party-meter__value  { color: var(--abyss-300); }

/*
    Buff icons are sprites, so the only way to desaturate one is to tint it.
*/
.party-member--away .buff-group__icon {
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--icon-muted);
}

.party-member--away .buff-group__fill {
    background-color: var(--abyss-600);
}

/*
    Replaces the rank badge's accent while the member is away, so leadership is
    still legible without being the brightest thing in a greyed row.
*/
.party-member--away .party-member__rank {
    background-color: var(--abyss-700);
    border-color: var(--abyss-400);
    color: var(--abyss-200);
}

.party-empty {
    margin-top: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
}

/* ── Footer ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */

.party-footer {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 34px;
    padding-left: 8px;
    padding-right: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.party-action {
    flex-grow: 1;
    height: 22px;
    margin-left: 2px;
    margin-right: 2px;
}
